  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms were ongoing for areas east of I-135 late \nthis morning and this activity is expected to gradually shift east \nand diminish as the mid-level waa continues to wane. Efficient \nrainfall is anticipated with PWATs over 2 inches with considerable \nwarm cloud depth approximations. Morning precipitation and some \ncloud debris lingering into the afternoon hours will likely limit \nmaximum temperatures for many areas this afternoon and values were \ntrended lower. This pattern will remain in place for tonight with \nanother rounds of showers and storms anticipated for much of the \narea. A shortwave trough emerging from the Northern Rockies may \nallow for better coverage of storms across the area tonight into \nearly Friday. The axis of heaviest precipitation may shift SSW \nslightly from the previous night with PWATs progged around 2.25 \ninches bringing efficient rainfall once again.\n\nThe deeper moisture in the column is progged to shift eastward as we \nmove through the day on Friday as we begin to see more influence \nfrom the mid/upper ridge over the Southern Rockies. A moist and \nuncapped airmass on Friday afternoon may lead to some afternoon \nstorms redeveloping along any residual outflow boundaries. Similar \nto today, morning precipitation and lingering cloud debris may \ndampen the diurnal rise on Friday, especially in eastern KS.\n\nAs we move through the weekend and into next week, rising \nheights/increasing thickness will support rising temperatures. \nHot and dry conditions are anticipated through much of the week \nbut as we move into the middle of the week, another frontal \nboundary will try to sag south into Kansas before becoming \nstationary. This front could become a focus for showers and \nstorms although higher probabilities are expected to remain \njust north of our area across northeast Kansas. Afternoon heat \nindices will likely climb over 100 through much of the week, \nespecially for southern and southeast Kansas.",
